    "alert_id": "EX3_001",
    "title": "Untreated hypothyroidism mimics DTG weight gain",
    "message": "Patient has TSH 12.4 mIU/L with low Free T4, dry skin, periorbital puffiness, delayed ankle reflexes, constipation, cold intolerance, and fatigue — classic hypothyroidism. She often forgets her levothyroxine. The clinician attributes 9 kg weight gain to DTG and plans to switch, but inadequately treated hypothyroidism is a compelling alternative cause. Correcting levothyroxine adherence and dose should be attempted before attributing weight gain solely to DTG. Switching away from DTG prematurely risks losing a well-tolerated, high-genetic-barrier regimen with suppressed viral load.",
    "issue_type": "plan_review",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Optimise levothyroxine adherence and dose, recheck TSH in 6–8 weeks before attributing weight gain to DTG or switching ART.",
    "citations": []
  },
